  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "The simplest form of \\(1-\\dfrac{1}{1+\\dfrac{a}{1-a}}\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(a\\) if \\(a\\ne0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(a\\) if \\(a\\ne-1\\)",
    "D": "\\(1-a\\) with no restriction on \\(a\\)",
    "E": "\\(a\\) if \\(a\\ne1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Begin with the innermost fraction, which requires \\(a\\ne1\\). For these values,\n\\[\n1+\\frac{a}{1-a}\n=\\frac{1-a+a}{1-a}\n=\\frac{1}{1-a}.\n\\]\nThis quantity is never zero, so the outer division introduces no additional restriction. Its reciprocal is \\(1-a\\), and therefore\n\\[\n1-\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{a}{1-a}}\n=1-(1-a)=a.\n\\]\nThe original expression remains undefined at \\(a=1\\), even though the simplified expression \\(a\\) alone is defined there. Thus the simplest form is \\(\\boxed{a\\text{ with }a\\ne1}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
